<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/classification-tree-of-life">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    With millions of species, biology needs a filing system that reflects <strong>actual
    ancestry</strong> — not convenience. Modern classification is a family tree (a
    <em>phylogeny</em>): species group by shared descent, and every named rank (domain, kingdom,
    phylum, class, order, family, genus, species) marks a branch point on it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from ladder to tree — the four facts that reorganised all of life" widget="equationStep" config={treeCfg} />

  <h2>Naming and counting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Binomial names:</strong> Homo sapiens, Escherichia coli — Linnaeus's two-word system (1753) is still the global standard, language-independent.</li>
    <li><strong>The scale is humbling:</strong> ~2.1 million species described; best estimates put the total at 8–10 million. Insects alone dwarf everything else; bacteria outweigh all plants and animals combined.</li>
    <li><strong>The microbial world dominates:</strong> by genes, most of the biosphere is bacterial and archaeal. Plants and animals are an evolutionary afterthought with excellent PR.</li>
    <li><strong>Extinction is part of the tree too:</strong> >99% of species that ever lived are gone. The current extinction rate — hundreds of times background — is pruning branches faster than we can name them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Woese, Kandler & Wheelis, PNAS (1990); OpenStax, Biology 2e (Diversity of Life); Catalogue of Life.</p>
</TopicPage>
